(R/'storage_reflection.md').write_text('# Storage reflection\n\nThe prior curriculum covers partial I/O, ownership and finite storage models, but a persistent engine requires a crash contract between them. A write can be accepted without persistence; a failed flush leaves an indeterminate result. The first implementation uses a bounded B+tree and one serialized writer, with no independent readers, one pending transaction and no uncommitted home-page writes. A full-page redo record and checksum are useful only under stated overwrite and corruption-detection assumptions. The old tree or complete new tree must survive every admitted cut; a clean reopen alone is not enough.\n\nThe held redo text supports ordered prepare/commit/write-back/reclaim stages. Its historical kernel barrier name is not copied into a current adapter. ARIES is retained as a foundational publication lead with an explicit uninspected algorithm boundary. B-tree evidence supports balance/split/merge reasoning; B+tree separators and linked leaves receive an original contract.\n',encoding='utf-8')
(R/'storage_synthesis.md').write_text('# Storage synthesis\n\nBuild one engine through small milestones: byte format, bounded clean cache, lookup/scan, split propagation, deletion repair/reuse, no-steal scratch, full-image WAL, checkpoint generation selection, recovery, injected failures, POSIX adapter and cross-family integration. The disk is preallocated; its creation/directory durability is outside the established-image recovery contract. A successful flush persists all prior page writes; unsuccessful operations may persist a subset in any order. Tears affect only the addressed page; post-flush media corruption and checksum collisions are outside the atomicity claim and must not be disguised as passed cases.\n\nTwo checksummed generation records retain one previous checkpoint while a newer one is installed. Committed WAL pages remain intact until home pages and the new checkpoint generation are flushed. Full-page replay is idempotent. Single-writer exclusion is an application obligation, not a lock implementation. Classify a failure before commit publication as aborted; after attempting commit publication as indeterminate, requiring reopen/recovery.\n',encoding='utf-8')
